#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134674} :activity/id #uuid "8da487d8-5da0-5730-90f5-0cc952b0b118" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049970 :topic/id #uuid "22979210-8cd9-5db4-861d-faf167d0a850" :topic/math-academy-id 2124} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135049 :tutorial/content "We've already seen how to find the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace in the case when the subspace is defined using an orthogonal basis. In this lesson, we'll learn how to do the same thing in the case of non-orthogonal bases.\n\nFor example, consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHow can we find the orthogonal projection $\\mathbf{x}_S$ of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}?$ Notice that since $\\mathbf a_1\\cdot \\mathbf a_2 \\neq 0,$ the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are not orthogonal.\n\nAs usual, we would like to write $\\mathbf{x}$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\in S$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\!\\in S^\\perp.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a5576fcaf9e0be6fc14978eb91dcbaceee75cbfddaa537fdb05d34b8e31e2e0.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp,$ that is, $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\perp S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expression for $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ into the system to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ (x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this system in an equivalent matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,{a}_{1}\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,{a}_{2}\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf we denote \n$[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]$\nand $[\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then we obtain\n\n$$\nA^TA\\mathbf{k} = A^T\\mathbf{x}\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{k}=(A^TA)^{-1}A^T\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nAlso, we note that  $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2$ can be expressed as $\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x}\t= A\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =Ak \\\\  & =A({A}^{T}A)^{-1}{A}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a formula for the orthogonal projection!\n\nNow, we return to our example. Using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: Pay attention to the order in which we carried out the multiplication. Carrying out the operations in the order shown above can save us a lot of work!" :tutorial/id #uuid "0a9a954f-9338-5c3f-a9db-9b39a1a60c0b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3626 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f3cce71c-fd0d-5040-949b-813f3fdcbdd7" :step/math-academy-id 20246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133788 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133597 :question/id #uuid "b8bbe0e1-8bf1-5b3a-a77e-6350ea77e83f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5575"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392354}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "8e0f305d-efdf-586e-b799-66fe2b5a8f6a" :step/math-academy-id 20247 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135050 :tutorial/content "If $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is a set of *linearly independent* vectors, then the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $A$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n,$ that is,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | & … & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & … & {a}_{n} \\\\ | & | & … & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The fact that $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ must be linearly independent is important. If the vectors are linearly dependent, we need to find the basis of the span before applying the formula." :tutorial/id #uuid "3274de58-5861-55c3-9614-08540ad4231e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3627 :tutorial/title "The General Formula for the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "c20a2e38-a98a-579e-9564-09d78a7f10c9" :step/math-academy-id 20248 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133789 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133598 :question/id #uuid "ac8363a8-fc77-52e0-b060-5e3a45c5171a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5576"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050727}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394738}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "184ad4b9-e662-5d0f-9c92-14f6b77fcc1f" :step/math-academy-id 20249 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135051 :tutorial/content "As we've already seen, the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the one-dimensional subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}} \\, \\mathbf{x} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}}{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}} \\: \\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\nIf we suppose that $\\mathbf{a}$ denotes the column-vector $[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ a \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]$ then the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is simply $\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a},$ and we can write the formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{(a\\cdot x)}{(a\\cdot a)}\\,a \\\\  & =[\\frac{({a}^{T}x)}{({a}^{T}a)}]a \\\\  & =a[\\frac{({a}^{T}x)}{({a}^{T}a)}] \\\\  & =a[({a}^{T}a)^{-1}{a}^{T}x] \\\\  & =a\\,({a}^{T}a)^{-1}{a}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compare the above with the formula that we use when we want to project onto an arbitrary subspace:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \nAs we can see, they are basically the same thing!" :tutorial/id #uuid "491ffb4a-98e5-59b5-8c01-3d23f872cc3f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3628 :tutorial/title "Some Intuition Behind the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "5b14c806-2358-52c9-aabe-db4086c3d1ef" :step/math-academy-id 20250}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Arbitrary Bases)" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134003}]}
